<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>

<section xml:id="section-limits-discover" >
<title>Discovering Limits</title>

	<subsection xml:id="subsection-develop-definition">
		<title>Developing a Definition</title>
		
		<p>This section is entitled <em>discovering</em> limits. Why is it discover rather than learn? This is designed to mimic mathematics in life. In life people typically notice a recurring trait in something (make an observation). They take time to describe the trait carefully, likely using many examples. Through this process of careful description, they construct a definition. This is to say that definitions are not printed on the universe: they are simply the result of people describing a phenomenon in a way that enables them to work with it.</p>

		<p>In this section we will practice this process of noticing a recurring trait and  constructing a definition. Because the examples below have been curated, we will end up noticing some important traits of functions that led over a couple hundred years to the modern definitions for limits.</p>
	</subsection>

	<subsection xml:id="subsection-nice-curve-limits">
		<title>Nicer Curves</title>
		
		<p>A major theme of this course is learning properties of curves that make them nice, that is in some context it is easier to work with them. The first of these properties is a limit being defined at a point, or, even nicer, the limit being defined on an interval. Defined on an interval means that the limit is defined at every point in that interval.</p>

		<p>In this section we are describing limits visually. We will work on a symbolic definition for use in calculations in other sections. For now we will be satisfied by being able to give visual descriptions of what functions (graphs) look like at a point or on an interval where the limit is defined.</p>
	</subsection>

	<subsection xml:id="subsection-discover-limit-def1">
		<title>Find a Pattern: Limits Defined</title>
		
		<p>Our first step in understanding when a limit is defined is to consider <xref ref="figure-limits-defined" /> which shows examples of points on curves where the limit is defined, and <xref ref="figure-limits-not-defined" /> which shows examples of points on curves where the limit is not defined. For now just ask yourself what you notice. Write this down.</p>

